+    /**
+     * Where the renderer puts the baseline inside an {@code w:lineRule="exact"} box that is
+     * SHORTER than the em, as a fraction of the font size rather than of the box.
+     *
+     * <p>{@link #LINE_BASE} is measured on boxes tall enough to hold their own em, and there
+     * the ratio follows the box. The corpus's flowing line is not that: it is the hairline
+     * {@code w:line = 1.38 * size} that makes Word and Google agree on the ADVANCE, i.e.
+     * 0.65 pt for a 10.5 pt line, so {@code LINE_BASE * box} is 0.52 pt. The renderer does not
+     * place the baseline 0.52 pt below a box top it then overflows; it places it a fraction of
+     * the EM, and that fraction does not shrink with the box.
+     *
+     * <p>Measured in Google, on {@code 31_times_body_arial_headings}, by matching every line of
+     * the export against the source PDF: the text sits a median +2.755 pt below where the page
+     * draws it (20 of 29 lines matched, range +0.58 to +3.59), while LibreOffice passes the same
+     * document 42/42. That is why the defect is invisible to the proxy.
+     *
+     * <p>The correction does not move the pitch. {@code w:before + w:line} is what advances the
+     * line, so this many twips are moved OUT of {@code w:before} and INTO {@code w:line}: the box
+     * top rises, the box grows by the same amount, the advance is unchanged, and the baseline -
+     * which the renderer places inside the box - rises with the box top. Measured the same way:
+     * 18_displacement FAIL -> PASS, 42/42, still one page.
+     */
+    private static final float BASELINE_EM =
+            Float.parseFloat(System.getProperty("p2w.baselineEm", "0.312"));
